

This might be the place for studying the fundamentals of MILPE algorithm..

==========================================================================================================================================================
"main01"
Objective: Reconstruction of cos(t) via linear combination of sin(t) and sin(t+1e-13).
Observation:  
  - First of all, this demo wasn't originally planned. Not sure either if the result is valuable.
  - Inputs and outputs are designed as simple as possible to explore the behavior of algorithm.
  - sin(t) alone can't represent cos(t) but it is possible if sin(t+1e-13) joins.
  - why...?
  - GPT answer:
      Mathematically, this happens because a tiny phase-shifted sine contains information about the derivative direction of sin(t), which is cos(t).
      the two input signals are almost identical, but their tiny difference contains the missing derivative information.
